 ## Overview
-Hierarchical DDA with Chunk Stepping traces a ray through a hierarchical voxel map by stepping through chunks using a 3D Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Hierarchical DDA](5710-hierarchical-dda.md) by treating the lowest hierarchy level as solid $8 \times 8 \times 8$ blocks, allowing the ray to skip empty regions in $O(1)$ time per chunk boundary [S1, unofficial]. The method detects solid chunks, converts the world-space entry point to chunk-local coordinates, steps through the voxel grid, and continues until a hit is found or map bounds are exceeded [S1, unofficial].
+This method traces a ray through a hierarchical voxel map by stepping through large "chunks" using a 3D Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm, detecting solid chunks, converting to local coordinates, and continuing the ray until a hit is found or bounds are exceeded [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Hierarchical DDA](12757-hierarchical-dda.md) by explicitly managing the transition between chunks with varying refractive indices and handling asynchronous chunk loading via flags [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard DDA by skipping empty space at the chunk level and from pure voxel marching by maintaining a hierarchical structure that allows for large-step traversal [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method maintains a DDA state to determine when the ray crosses a chunk boundary [S1, unofficial]. Upon entering a solid chunk, it computes the exact entry point, converts it to local coordinates, and steps through the $8 \times 8 \times 8$ voxel grid [S1, unofficial]. If the chunk is not solid or has a pending load flag, the algorithm marks it for asynchronous loading and advances the DDA to the next chunk [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs:** Ray origin ($rayPos$), ray direction ($rayDir$), inverse ray direction ($invRayDir$), map bounds, chunk size ($CHUNK\_SIZE$), and a hierarchical map data structure containing chunk metadata and voxel data.
+**Outputs:** A hit result containing the world position, local coordinate, and normal of the first intersected voxel, or a failure state if the ray exits the map or exceeds the range.
 
